WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.540
Credit union membership is growing.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.580
But most credit unions aren't sharing
in that growth.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.280
New numbers show the gap.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.040
Also this week: AI moves deeper into
everyday banking, and a payments

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.040
project gets a clearer path to launch.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.140
This is CUAI Weekly.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.320
Let's start with the membership
picture.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:22.080
The NCUA released its second quarter
state level report on September

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.300
23rd. Across federally insured credit
unions, membership rose

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.620
overall. Yet about 56% of institutions
had fewer members than a

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.340
year earlier. Those two things can
happen together.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.960
Growth across the system doesn't mean
growth at the typical institution.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.440
The median membership change was a
decline of 0.6%.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.260
Among credit unions losing members,
more than half had less than $50

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.020
million in assets. Geography mattered,
too.

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:50.980
Vermont and Alaska led median
membership growth.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.420
Arkansas and Nebraska had the steepest
median declines.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:58.929
These are year over year comparisons
through June, not a reading

00:00:58.929 --> 00:01:02.740
of September activity. For a leadership
team, the useful question is where

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:04.900
your own institution sits within that
picture.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.160
Compare your membership trend with
similar institutions before treating the

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.780
national growth headline as evidence
that your market is healthy.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:13.840
The report describes the divide;

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.960
it doesn't establish what's causing it.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.960
Now, to AI inside the working day.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.620
Narmi announced an AI Marketplace on
September 24th, bringing its banking

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:24.880
tools into one hub.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.380
The company says the available features
include account opening review

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.520
assistance, message drafting, marketing
tools, and natural language search.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.680
One example is DecisionAssist.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.900
It summarizes identity verification
information and makes recommendations

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.800
for staff reviewing new accounts.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.040
Narmi reports that average review time
fell from eight minutes to less than

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.920
two. That's a vendor reported result,
not an independently verified

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.960
benchmark or a promise for every
institution.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.280
The distinction between available
features and the roadmap matters here.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.520
Narmi lists document agents and agentic
secure messaging as upcoming

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.680
capabilities. They shouldn't be treated
as already delivered.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.820
If you're evaluating tools like these,
ask to see the workflow with your

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:10.100
own approval rules: what information
the model sees, what the employee

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.200
checks, and how an error gets
corrected.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:16.120
A faster review is valuable only if the
decision remains reliable.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.420
Finally, a development in the plumbing
behind payments.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.980
The Clearing House selected Quant to
support its On Chain Money Initiative.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:28.220
The planned network would let
participating institutions clear and settle

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.840
tokenized deposits: digital
representations of deposits, moved on a

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:33.680
different kind of ledger.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.160
The announcement describes connections
to existing payment systems,

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.520
including RTP and CHIPS.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.660
It targets availability in the first
half of 2027.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.520
That's a planned launch, not a service
every credit union can switch on

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.160
today. Participation details are still
to come.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.800
For credit union payments teams, this
is a development to track alongside

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.620
existing real time payment options.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:59.200
Ask about access, integration, and the
member or business problem it would

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:00.920
actually solve before committing
resources.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.880
Those are three developments from the
week ending September 27th.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.980
You'll find the original reporting and
sources with this briefing.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.660
For more industry coverage, visit
Credit Union AI News.
